---

<BaseLayout
  title="Sweet vs Hot (Low-Heat) Paprika: What's the Difference | Tenda Peppers"
  description="Sweet paprika (under 500 SHU) vs low-heat paprika (under 10,000 SHU) — varieties, ASTA colour and when each grade is used. China manufacturer, ISO 9001 & HACCP."
  ogType="article"
  author="Tenda Peppers Technical Team"
  datePublished="2026-09-23"
  dateModified="2026-09-23"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  multilingual={false}
>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> &rsaquo; Sweet vs Hot Paprika
    </nav>

    <h1>Sweet vs Hot (Low-Heat) Paprika: What's the Difference</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Paprika comes in two grades: sweet, for colour with no burn, and low-heat, for colour plus mild heat and chilli aroma. This comparison explains the SHU split, the varieties behind each, and how to specify the grade you need.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Sweet paprika (under 500 SHU) is made from high-colour sweet pepper varieties like Xinjiang sweet pepper and Red Dragon 23, with an ASTA colour value of 80–180. Low-heat paprika (under 10,000 SHU) comes from varieties like Yidu red and Wangdu, adding mild heat and clear chilli aroma on top of colour.
    </div>
    <AtAGlance facts={glanceFacts} />

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The core difference</h2>
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #d8c9c0;text-align:left;">
          <th style="padding:8px;">Dimension</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Sweet paprika</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Low-heat paprika</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Heat (SHU)</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Under 500</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Under 10,000</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Varieties</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Xinjiang sweet pepper, Red Dragon 23</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Yidu red, Wangdu</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Colour (ASTA)</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">80–180 (sweet grade)</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">60–240 range, variety-dependent</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Character</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Colour, no burn</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Colour + mild heat + chilli aroma</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why the choice matters</h2>
    <p>
      Both grades come from low-heat, high-colour sweet peppers — the <a href="/products/paprika-powder/">paprika powder</a> page details the grades. Sweet paprika (under 500 SHU) is bought for colour and mild flavour with essentially no burn. Low-heat paprika (under 10,000 SHU) adds mild heat and a clear chilli aroma on top of the colour. Neither is interchangeable with <a href="/guides/paprika-vs-chilli-powder/">chilli powder</a>, which is made from hotter varieties (20,000–70,000+ SHU) to add heat.
    </p>
    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">When to choose each</h2>
    <p>
      Choose sweet paprika for colouring where heat is unwanted — natural red colouring, colour blending, mild snacks. Choose low-heat paprika where the product needs colour plus a mild warmth and chilli aroma on top.
    </p>
    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How to specify</h2>
    <p>
      Confirm your target ASTA colour value and heat level when you inquire — the supply range is roughly ASTA 60–240 depending on variety and grade. See <a href="/guides/asta-colour-value/">ASTA colour value</a> for how colour is measured.
    </p>
    <p class="data-note">
      Heat and colour values are representative. Confirm your exact SHU window, ASTA, form and mesh
      when you inquire.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is the difference between sweet paprika and low-heat paprika?</h3>
      <p>Sweet paprika (under 500 SHU) is made from high-colour sweet pepper varieties like Xinjiang sweet pepper and Red Dragon 23, with an ASTA colour value of 80–180. Low-heat paprika (under 10,000 SHU) comes from varieties like Yidu red and Wangdu, adding mild heat and clear chilli aroma on top of colour.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Can low-heat paprika replace sweet paprika?</h3>
      <p>Only if the added heat and chilli aroma are acceptable in your product. Sweet paprika is under 500 SHU; low-heat paprika is under 10,000 SHU. Confirm your target colour value and heat level when you inquire.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Which paprika is best for food colouring?</h3>
      <p>High-colour sweet pepper varieties from Xinjiang — such as Red Dragon 23 and US Red — give the strongest colour per unit, and are the standard for paprika oleoresin extraction and food colouring.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:32px;">
      <a class="cta" href="/products/paprika-powder/">Browse Paprika Powder</a>
      <a class="cta cta-outline" href="/contact/" style="margin-left:8px;">Request a Quote</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
